Z||000015||KissMySSFiero||02-06-2001||01:39 AM||||one placeof concern i would haveis the rear and front facia.  gt's you wouldnt have a problem with the front because there is no molding. however you still have it in the rear.  itslate and i cant hardly see, but i was editing some pics of my car and thats the only place i would see a problem.<P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:KissMySSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:KissMySSFiero@netzero.net">KissMySSFiero@netzero.net</A>   <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">       <BR>"There's a fine line between love and beer"<BR><A HREF="http://expage.com/kissmyssfiero" TARGET=_blank>KissMySSFiero.mywebsite.hehe</A>||||reg||1
Z||000016||rogergarrison||02-06-2001||07:41 AM||||first off youd have to fill that gap with something solid, maybe plastic bar. Just a filler that thick to fill the opening would crack back out pretty fast. Once you got the gap pretty filled , you could use the flexible plastic type filler to blend and smooth it in. You would have to adapt another gm door handle to fit i think too. it would take a while but definatly doable. I think doing it with glass matt wouldnt be worth all the extra work. You would have to grind out a recess all the way around the top and bottom of grove to make it set flush. then end up with body filler to smooth it out a foot wide all around. I think Id be tempted to try it on an old fender or door panel before i went for the car.||||reg||1
Z||000017||fieroshop||02-06-2001||10:53 AM||||Looks good, but has anybody considered that the filler would syrt to crack in short on the urathane panels. Since not much will stick to them they will start to crack. Another problem would be that the door panels are also attched to the door via the clips that hold the moulding in place, removing these will make the door skin vibrate and coause the filler to crack. We tried that on a door and it did not work. I would not be inclined to do this mod and have do redo it all again unless you do this mod for the looks and not drive the car but make it into a show car and trailered every where.<P>------------------<BR> <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||loafer87gt||02-06-2001||11:23 AM||||I would like to try to see how difficult it would to use the body trim from a Cavalier on a Fiero. The trim wouldn't fit inside the gap like on the fiero, but it is almost flat with a slight curve to it that I think would look sharp. Kinda tough to explain, next time you see a cavalier check out the trim strips. ||||reg||1
Z||000019||loafer87gt||02-06-2001||11:26 AM||||Heres the link to the last thread on this topic<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005257.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005257.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||stsmithgt||02-06-2001||11:44 AM||||I really like the smooth look.  That trim has  a function though.  A lot of trim is there to protect from door dings.  These hide seams. <I>I know you guys know this </I>.  There seems to be a lot more variables involved making this car seamless, diferent plastics and compound to deal with.  I lack the information, tools and location to even think about taking on a project like that at this time.  Good luck to the ones that do take on this challange. Your car can end up looking better than a lot of ours.  Right now I'm glad mine is still like a lego toy. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/legotoy.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||rogergarrison||02-06-2001||02:05 PM||||<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by loafer87gt:<BR><B>I would like to try to see how difficult it would to use the body trim from a Cavalier on a Fiero. The trim wouldn't fit inside the gap like on the fiero, but it is almost flat with a slight curve to it that I think would look sharp. Kinda tough to explain, next time you see a cavalier check out the trim strips. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>thats a piece of cake. all the moulding on Cavalier are stuck on with 2 sided tape. You can cut the ends off real clean using tree pruning sheers. would be a little tough getting it clean around door handle.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000022||jerpro||02-06-2001||02:53 PM||||very simple, All you have to do is grab the actuall panel moldings and grind them down to the panel surface, then when it is all leveled you can finish off with the surface glassing. That way you keep the vibration off the door (you stull have original moldings) and you can smooth it out. PRetty time consuming, but when you want to do something right it takes some time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Only try your best, and if at first you don't succeed... TRY HARDER.||||reg||1
